From: bob_g@eris.demon.co.uk (Robert D Grahame)
Date: Wed, 06 Jul 94 19:58:01  GMT
Subject: [#94-7-28] Possible new Extropian book.

I haven't finished reading this yet, but I'm liking it so far :-
"The Collapse of Chaos" by Jack Cohen and Ian Stewart, Viking, 1994
ISBN 0-670-84983-9, 18.00ukp hardback (avail from Pengiun Books in the USA).
Subtitle : 'Discovering simplicity in a complex world.'

A couple of quotes which might hook Extropian interests "There is no
inexorable trend toward increasing disorder" (albeit in a special case), but
more generally, "[...] the relentless increase of entropy does not apply to a
'free-running' system - such as the entire universe." (!)

It spends the first half of the book covering old ground (just to make sure
everyone is up to speed) on standard reductionist science, evolution, chaos
and complexity theory. It also covers the background of the current philosphy
of science, Occam, the Copenhagen interpretation of QM, etc.

Then it goes back over the same ground again from a rather different
perspective. It's view is, roughly, that when scientists have tried to answer
big 'why' questions, they have often used the same mental models that are so
successful in normal 'how does this bit work' science, and that this has lead
to a several dead ends that we are only just beginning to realise *are* dead
ends. Schrodingers cat and the normal extrapolation of the laws of
thermodynamics (that the universe will always tend to disorder) both get
short shrift. I'm not quite convinced yet, but it's looking good.

Both authors are scientists, and the book is not in any way an attack on
reductionist science, but rather a pointer to where the standard reductionist
toolkit has sometimes been used inappropriately. It shows some better
approaches that are still 'hard' science. No wooly headed holism here. The
book does use occasional dialogues with fictional and historical characters
to lighten the tone and get some points across, a bit like the style of
Hofstadter's GEB. An octimal book, as Neeplphut would say... :-)

Has anyone else here read it?

