Subject: [#94-7-30] INFO REQUEST: chording/folding keyboards

There is a wide variety of information about keyboards, software, medical
procedures, exercises, etc. available in

        ftp://rtfm.mit.edu/pub/usenet/news.answers/typing-injury-faq

It is also posted periodically to sci.med.occupational.

I'm posting this to the list since many people are likely to be interested.
I myself had a severe case of forearm tendinitis a couple of years ago. It
has pretty much cleared up, but only after six months of physiotherapy, and
a lot of anxiety for a time when I wasn't sure whether it was going to go
away. The important things I learned from this are:

- Act as soon as possible, preferably before you notice anything wrong; if
you ignore the pain for long enough, you can cause irreversible damage

- Take frequent, brief rest breaks (e.g. 1 min in 20)

- Get a light touch keyboard, arrange it ergonomically, and don't hit the
keys any harder than you need to

- Do moderate exercise to strengthen and stretch the relevant muscles and
tendons (don't overdo this if you are already feeling signs of strain)

- Anti-inflammatories are a good temporary relief, but they have serious
side effects with long term use

This was enough to take care of the problem for me, without any additional
hardware. (Which would have been difficult to install in my notebook
computer, in any case. I did choose the computer in part because it had a
soft touch keyboard and a wrist rest, and a comfortable side mounted trackball.)

Good luck!

