Subject: [#94-7-31] Extropy Journal Volume

At  6:38 PM 7/5/94 -0700, Jim Stevenson wrote:
>Libertarians say the free market will solve problems like helping the
>handicaped.
>Well, can all you lib types demonstrate this?

So the arrival of the free market will raise the dead, cure the lepers, and
make the blind see, huh? I'm looking forward to it.

Seriously, while I suspect that a healthy, growing economy is better for
the handicapped (as it's better for most everyone), I don't think the case
for libertarianism has to rest on the market's ability to solve such
problems. Think of our present rent-seeking society as a big prisoner's
dilemma. We'd all be better off if everyone refrained from special
pleading, but individually our incentives are the opposite.

What's this got to do with solving people's problems? Just this. Claiming
that the market will solve a specific problem better than rent-seeking is
only one argument for the market. It's possible to imagine a market-based
society that handles the problem of handicaps worse than the status quo,
but in which handicapped people are still better off (gains in other areas
compensate for their more obvious losses).

The market can still be the best alternative without it being a magic wand.
I do believe that medical and technological advances that can help or even
cure handicaps will appear sooner under a market system. I have no airtight
demonstration of this, but neither does the opposition.

-- Dave                       tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)



