Subject: [#94-7-36] Nanotechnology and uncertainty

hibbert@netcom.com writes

>The weakness in your argument is that you assume that someone will get some
>discrete jump in ability ....  What actually happens in the real world
>it that everyone moves forward by steps, and so in the year in which someone
>invents a 1000 brainpower machine, some of the other competitors will have
>invented 900 brainpower machines 3 months earlier, and others will come out
>with
>1500 brainpower machines 3 months later.  No one gets to be a factor of 1000
>ahead
>of everyone else at any point.

AI, as opposed to most other technologies, has a curious self-accelerating
effect.  As soon as someone develops a machine that is better, faster or
cheaper at designing AI systems than is a human designer, they can use that
machine to develop the next generation of such machines, which leads to a
self-accelerating cycle culminating in the creation of godlike intellects,
that are to us as we are to the beasts that perish.

Imagine, for example, that a design team of 100 people spends two years
designing the first slightly superhuman AI.  They start work in January
2010, and in January 2012 they have a machine that is as good at designing
hardware and software as they are, but does it twice as fast.  The humans
build a hundred of these Mark I gizmos, hook them together, and they design
the Mark II, which is four times as fast as a human.  The Mark II is
available in January 2013, and it designs the Mark III, available in
August.  By New Year's Eve 2013, they're up to the Mark VII, with 16 KB
(kilobrain) capacity, and new generations are emerging every few hours.

Another project, that pursues a seperate development path, might be only
one month behind the leaders, but that translates to many generations once
development accelerates.  They would be hopelessly behind.  If the
development occurs in an open environment, the lagging groups can catch up
by buying machines from the leaders, poaching trade secrets, reading
scientific publications, and so on.  However, the advantage in keeping
developments secret would be very large, so I suspect the lead group would
not publicize its methods.

Of course, the development rate cannot become infinite; eventually it will
be limited by some resource other than the cleverness of design.  But I
suspect that it is feasible to develop machines that think a million times
faster than humans.  Such machines could change the world beyond
recognition in a matter of hours.  To return to the topic of military
uncertainty, this would have a destabilizing effect on war (and everything
else).

Note that this scenario is independent of the level of nanotechnology
available at the time of the development of AI.  If it's already available,
fine.  If not initial developments will be slowed slightly but eventually
the machines will create it, sometime around Mark XVI.

Speaking aa a human being, I find this scenario alarming.




